[32mAnswer #2: Japan's Lost Decades can be understood through the lens of institutional rigidity and delayed structural transformation amid a rapidly changing global economy. Unlike my previous explanation focusing on financial mechanisms, this perspective emphasizes how Japan's postwar economic model became increasingly incompatible with emerging realities.

The Japanese economic miracle (1950s-1980s) relied on an industrial policy where government, banks, and keiretsu (business groups) coordinated closely. This developmental state model excelled during catch-up industrialization but proved ill-suited for an innovation-driven, service-oriented economy.

Japan's political economy featured powerful vested interests that resisted necessary reforms:
- The electoral system favored rural constituencies over urban centers
- Bureaucratic ministries protected established industries
- Lifetime employment systems impeded labor mobility
- Regulatory frameworks privileged incumbents over new entrants

When the crisis hit, these institutional rigidities prevented creative destruction and economic renewal. While other advanced economies were embracing digitalization, services, and flexible work arrangements, Japan's transition stalled.

This perspective explains why multiple reform attempts failed—the political system itself was designed to preserve stability rather than facilitate disruptive change, creating a prolonged period of economic underperformance despite Japan's considerable technological and human capital advantages.[0m
