[32mAnswer #8: Japan's Lost Decades can be understood through the lens of political economy and factional politics that undermined effective crisis response. Japan's political system, dominated by the Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) for most of the post-war period, created structural impediments to decisive economic reform.

The LDP's factional structure (habatsu) prioritized internal power-balancing and patronage distribution over coherent economic management. Policy formation required consensus among competing factions, producing watered-down compromises rather than bold initiatives. This system functioned adequately during high-growth decades but proved disastrous during crisis when decisive action was needed.

Political fragmentation intensified during the 1990s with brief opposition rule and LDP coalition governments, creating policy discontinuity. Economic ministries often worked at cross-purposes—the Ministry of Finance prioritized fiscal consolidation while METI focused on industrial protection. This bureaucratic fragmentation prevented coordinated responses to the crisis.

