When reformist leaders like Koizumi attempted structural changes in the early 2000s, factional resistance limited implementation and successors quickly reversed course. This political perspective explains why Japan's economic problems persisted—its political system was structurally incapable of sustaining the comprehensive reforms necessary for economic renewal.[0m
