The concentration of assets among older households created a structural demand problem. Japan's wealth became increasingly concentrated among risk-averse seniors with low consumption propensities, while younger generations with higher consumption tendencies had diminished earnings prospects and limited asset accumulation.

This inequality perspective explains why aggregate demand remained persistently weak despite policy interventions—Japan's economic problem wasn't simply insufficient total wealth but its increasingly unproductive distribution across society, creating a stratified economy unable to generate the broad-based consumption and innovation needed for sustained recovery.[0m
